Q: i don`t understand where is the memoery leak A: <p><code>your text</code>In analyzing your code and noting the good comments, the block of code in question lies within the condition where the hash table address value is not NULL.</p>
<pre><code>    strcpy(new_node -&gt; word, word);
    int x = hash(new_node -&gt; word);
    if (hashtable[x] == NULL)
    {
        hashtable[x] = new_node;
        hashtable[x] -&gt; next = NULL;

    }
    else
    {
        hashtable[x] -&gt; next = new_node;    /* This can potentially orphan a memory address     */
        new_node = hashtable[x];
    }
</code></pre>
<p>With the manner being used in this code example of deriving a hash value (one per each letter in the alphabet), if the dictionary loading process attempts to allocate memory and set up the hash table linked list, only the first word and the last word for a starting letter would be stored in the hash table list.  Previously allocated memory references would be lost.  To illustrate this, a simple dictionary with twenty-two words is loaded, having four words start with the letter 'a'.</p>
<pre><code>aid
angel
apple
axis
</code></pre>
<p>Connecting these functions to a simple main function that loads the dictionary, checks for misspelled words, and then unloads the dictionary will not free up the lost memory addresses.  Following is a sample run with the terminal output.  Note that the words &quot;angel&quot; and &quot;APPLE&quot; were in the &quot;Tests.txt&quot; file.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Spell_7/bin/Release$ ./Spell_7 Stuff.txt Tests.txt
Addresses allocated: 22

MISSPELLED WORDS

angel       &lt;--- erroneously not found - address has been orphaned 
APPLE       &lt;--- erroneously not found - address has been orphaned 
ideal
load
nope
state
Addresses freed: 20     &lt;--- only 20 memory addresses freed, not 22
</code></pre>
<p>To address this issue, the code within the &quot;else&quot; block, is refactored as follows:</p>
<pre><code>    strcpy(new_node -&gt; word, word);
    int x = hash(new_node -&gt; word);
    if (hashtable[x] == NULL)
    {
        hashtable[x] = new_node;
        hashtable[x] -&gt; next = NULL;

    }
    else
    {
        new_node-&gt; next = hashtable[x];     /* Put new node at the beginning of hash table  */
        hashtable[x] = new_node;
    }
</code></pre>
<p>This refactoring sets the &quot;next&quot; node pointer for the newly created node structure to the current first node structure address, and then reassigns the first node structure address to the new node structure address.</p>
<p>With that bit of refactoring, following was a retest of the program with the following terminal output.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Spell_7/bin/Release$ ./Spell_7 Stuff.txt Tests.txt
Addresses allocated: 22

MISSPELLED WORDS

ideal
load
nope
state
Addresses freed: 22     &lt;--- correct number of addresses freed
</code></pre>
<p>Now, the words &quot;angel&quot; and &quot;APPLE&quot; are not listed as &quot;false positives&quot; as they are found in the node list, and the same amount of memory is being freed as was allocated.</p>
<p>The takeaway here is to delve further in the study of linked lists and trees as it pertains to referencing one address to the next.</p>

Q: How to simulate target=&quot;_blank&quot; in JavaScript A: <p>To avoid pop-up blockers to block the window, you can create an &quot;a&quot; element in a function and call it with the url you want to use as param :</p>
<pre><code>const openExternalLink = (url: string) =&gt; {
    const a = document.createElement('a');
    a.href = url;
    a.target = '_blank';
    a.rel = 'noopener noreferrer';
    a.click();
}
</code></pre>

Q: Configuring AWS credentials not authorized to perform sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity A: <p>I had this same problem in my lab while trying to deploy to ECS via GitHub Actions with OIDC.</p>
<p>I figured AWS would reject the connection if the branch or repo name in <code>sub</code> does not match the token from GitHub. See my trusted condition entries below, and <a href="https://i.sstatic.net/Umq0LphE.jpg" rel="nofollow noreferrer">my deployment was successful.</a></p>
<pre><code>&quot;Condition&quot;: {
  &quot;StringEquals&quot;: {
    &quot;token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud&quot;: &quot;sts.amazonaws.com&quot;,
    &quot;token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub&quot;: &quot;repo:GitHubOrg/GitHubRepo:ref:refs/heads/GitHubBranch&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The value in <code>aud</code> is wrong; it should just be <code>&quot;sts.amazonaws.com&quot;</code>. <a href="https://i.sstatic.net/MBPE81lp.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Got this from the AWS docs on OIDC</a>.</p>
<p><a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_create_for-idp_oidc.html#idp_oidc_Create_GitHub" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_create_for-idp_oidc.html#idp_oidc_Create_GitHub</a></p>

