Q: Why does OnAudioFilterRead(float[], int) not work when I try to override the data parameter with a float[] of the same length? A: <p>In <strong>C#</strong>, an <strong>array</strong> is a <strong>reference type</strong>, so you need to modify the existing array values directly. Your original code fails because using the new keyword creates a separate object in memory, causing your local data variable to lose its connection to the original buffer. To fix this, you must keep the original reference and instead use <code>System.Array.Clear(data, 0, data.Length)</code> to wipe it before writing. Also you should use the &quot;+=&quot; operator to layer multiple frequencies into that same buffer (..if you use &quot;=&quot;, each frequency in your loop will overwrite the previous one, and you will only hear the very last note in your array). Additionally, you must maintain a separate phase for each frequency to ensure a clean signal and divide the final output by the number of frequencies to prevent digital clipping.</p>
<pre><code>private double[] _phases; // Separate phase for each frequency

public void PlaySineWave(float[] frequency, float amplitude, float[] data, int channels)
{
    // Modify the existing buffer directly
    System.Array.Clear(data, 0, data.Length);

    if (_phases == null || _phases.Length != frequency.Length)
        _phases = new double[frequency.Length];

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; frequency.Length; i++)
    {
        double phaseIncrement = frequency[i] / sampleRate;

        for (int sample = 0; sample &lt; data.Length; sample += channels)
        {
            float value = Mathf.Sin((float)_phases[i] * 2 * Mathf.PI) * amplitude * saturation;
            _phases[i] = (_phases[i] + phaseIncrement) % 1;

            for (int chan = 0; chan &lt; channels; chan++)
            {
                data[sample + chan] += value / frequency.Length;
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
